1
00:00:13,240 --> 00:00:18,842
[SPEAKER_02]: So, this is from the Schroeder note on kamma, from the second paragraph.

2
00:00:20,563 --> 00:00:25,605
[SPEAKER_02]: The question of kamma, or action, what should I do, is the ethical question.

3
00:00:26,026 --> 00:00:33,009
[SPEAKER_02]: For all personal action, all action done by me, is either akusala or kusala, unskillful or skillful.

4
00:00:33,895 --> 00:00:47,420
[SPEAKER_02]: And skillful action is rooted in lobha-dosa-moha, or lust-hate-delusion, and, apart from resulting in future dukkha and unpleasure, leads to the arising of action, not to the cessation of action.

5
00:00:48,860 --> 00:00:57,327
[SPEAKER_02]: Skillful action is rooted in non-lust, non-hate and non-delusion, and leads to the cessation of action, not to the arising of action.

6
00:00:58,047 --> 00:01:04,352
[SPEAKER_02]: The patujana does not understand this, since he sees neither the arising nor cessation of action.

7
00:01:04,372 --> 00:01:10,837
[SPEAKER_02]: The ditthi-sampanna does understand this, since he sees both the arising and cessation of action.

8
00:01:11,922 --> 00:01:16,424
[SPEAKER_02]: The arahant not only understands this, but has also reached the cessation of action.

9
00:01:16,864 --> 00:01:21,606
[SPEAKER_02]: Since for him, the question, what should I do, no more arises.

10
00:01:21,846 --> 00:01:26,367
[SPEAKER_02]: To the extent there is still intention in the case of the arahant, there is still conscious action.

11
00:01:26,908 --> 00:01:32,650
[SPEAKER_02]: But since it is neither unskillful nor skillful, it is no longer the action in the ethical sense.

12
00:01:33,590 --> 00:01:39,152
[SPEAKER_02]: Extinction, nibbāna, is the cessation of ethics.

13
00:01:40,953 --> 00:01:41,253
[SPEAKER_02]: So,

14
00:01:43,822 --> 00:01:54,947
[SPEAKER_02]: What interested me with this was the idea of things leading to more action or less action.

15
00:01:55,027 --> 00:01:58,589
[SPEAKER_02]: And that's kind of what the patujana needs to understand.

16
00:01:58,609 --> 00:02:03,751
[SPEAKER_02]: It's a matter then, I guess, of how does one... what is the distinction between those two?

17
00:02:03,811 --> 00:02:07,953
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, what is it then between greed, hatred, delusion,

18
00:02:08,573 --> 00:02:15,215
[SPEAKER_02]: Non-greed, non-hatred, non-delusion, that does lead to this cessation of action.

19
00:02:15,335 --> 00:02:19,136
[SPEAKER_02]: Like, what is, what is that, what is, what is special, different between them?

20
00:02:19,717 --> 00:02:25,519
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, eh... Before, like, one thing to point out, point out is, eh...

21
00:02:26,099 --> 00:02:31,080
[SPEAKER_01]: The problem with, with Pratījana and the notion of action is that, eh...

22
00:02:33,361 --> 00:02:36,162
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot see the negative aspect of a positive action.

23
00:02:37,245 --> 00:02:38,046
[SPEAKER_01]: They cannot see the...

24
00:02:38,626 --> 00:02:45,810
[SPEAKER_01]: So, let's say, action A is done, then you replace that by doing something else, as in, you do an action B.

25
00:02:46,611 --> 00:02:58,799
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you replace that by doing something else, by... you do action C. What Puthujjana needs to start discerning is that you don't want to replace action A with action B.

26
00:02:59,519 --> 00:03:06,964
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to be able to develop a non-action of not A, as in absence of action A.

27
00:03:08,062 --> 00:03:11,784
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the definition of sīla and virtue being done rightly.

28
00:03:12,325 --> 00:03:15,006
[SPEAKER_01]: In the beginning, people take on the precepts and they do them.

29
00:03:15,586 --> 00:03:19,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're not actively doing anything, your attitude is of doing it, keeping it.

30
00:03:20,549 --> 00:03:27,273
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you have been developed in virtue correctly, then those precepts start keeping you, so to speak.

31
00:03:27,934 --> 00:03:36,879
[SPEAKER_01]: In other words, you recognize that it's not about doing sīla, it's more about not doing non-sīla, not doing non-virtue.

32
00:03:38,585 --> 00:03:47,573
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason, that's why the sīla is a necessary precondition for any understanding, is because it emulates the principle, the principle of overcoming the unwholesome.

33
00:03:49,014 --> 00:03:57,702
[SPEAKER_01]: So, initially, of course, a putujana just needs to abstain from unwholesome, abstain from unwholesome action, speech and thought.

34
00:03:58,683 --> 00:04:04,248
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's only there to create a basis for understanding, and that understanding is when you see

35
00:04:04,857 --> 00:04:13,801
[SPEAKER_01]: The negative aspect, as in the peripheral, the general, the general nature of the unwholesome, and you stop entertaining the unwholesome.

36
00:04:15,301 --> 00:04:23,805
[SPEAKER_01]: And that general nature becomes apparent when you realize that unwholesome is concerned with the content of your thought, not the presence of that content of the thought.

37
00:04:25,406 --> 00:04:31,088
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that manner, you can cultivate non-unwholesome, which is automatically wholesome.

38
00:04:33,564 --> 00:04:47,074
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you're saying, then, that the wrong thing to do is kind of trying to make yourself wholesome, so to speak, by doing good ethical things that people agree is good and wholesome and stuff.

39
00:04:47,534 --> 00:04:47,894
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

40
00:04:47,934 --> 00:04:49,635
[SPEAKER_01]: There's nothing wrong with it.

41
00:04:50,316 --> 00:04:51,637
[SPEAKER_01]: It's nothing unwholesome to do that.

42
00:04:52,177 --> 00:04:56,060
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's wrong to think that that's the way of leading you out of wholesome and unwholesome.

43
00:04:58,154 --> 00:05:05,358
[SPEAKER_01]: You get out of it by not engaging with unwholesome and then discerning that absence of unwholesome is what wholesome is.

44
00:05:06,079 --> 00:05:11,562
[SPEAKER_01]: Ultimately, wholesome in the sense that pertains to the freedom from both wholesome and unwholesome.

45
00:05:12,542 --> 00:05:24,129
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you recognize that you don't need to do something, all you need to do is avoid doing certain things, as in avoid the unwholesome, that in itself is wholesome.

46
00:05:25,981 --> 00:05:29,403
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a negative action in that sense, which means you can't really call it an action.

47
00:05:30,284 --> 00:05:36,128
[SPEAKER_01]: Abstaining, abstaining from unwholesome action, if done rightly, it's not an action.

48
00:05:40,173 --> 00:05:50,838
[SPEAKER_02]: So, is that how it is, then, that when he says the arahant, you know, the arahants obviously still do things, they don't just become rocks or blocks of wood.

49
00:05:50,858 --> 00:05:50,978
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

50
00:05:51,558 --> 00:05:55,020
[SPEAKER_02]: But there's no action there in the sense that we're speaking.

51
00:05:55,140 --> 00:05:55,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

52
00:05:55,660 --> 00:06:00,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Because action was on the level of that assumption of doing, either doing good or doing bad.

53
00:06:01,163 --> 00:06:02,763
[SPEAKER_01]: That is fueled by greed, aversion, delusion.

54
00:06:02,803 --> 00:06:03,784
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fueled by desire.

55
00:06:05,404 --> 00:06:06,385
[SPEAKER_01]: That's fueled by craving.

56
00:06:07,787 --> 00:06:09,448
[SPEAKER_02]: Even for wholesome, like creative...

57
00:06:09,468 --> 00:06:10,309
[SPEAKER_01]: Even for wholesome, yeah.

58
00:06:10,689 --> 00:06:14,031
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why some of these people are, because I'm not free from desire, I won't do anything wholesome.

59
00:06:14,051 --> 00:06:14,651
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's a mistake.

60
00:06:14,671 --> 00:06:19,934
[SPEAKER_01]: You have no choice, but to do whatever you do out of a desire.

61
00:06:20,475 --> 00:06:33,503
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep doing good, then, on account of avoiding bad, because then, on account of doing good, you can see the abstaining from doing both bad and now this good, is that negative phenomena behind the action that you can discern.

62
00:06:34,803 --> 00:06:37,425
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, if, say, being immoral is A,

63
00:06:38,385 --> 00:06:40,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Being moral is B.

64
00:06:42,067 --> 00:06:46,630
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you then do B, but it's still done as an action.

65
00:06:47,851 --> 00:07:01,979
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, on top of it, if you want to go beyond, and you can go only beyond of being being moral, you can then use that action to understand the nature of action, to transcend the content of action, as I was describing in the previous video.

66
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:06,582
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have stopped doing the unwholesome, and now your act is purely

67
00:07:07,025 --> 00:07:13,231
[SPEAKER_01]: Virtuous, kept by the priesthood, seeing the danger in the slightest fault, your action is action B, that is a good action.

68
00:07:13,632 --> 00:07:20,679
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, on top of that, you start then discerning, okay, so I don't need to go and do action C now, nor do I need to revert to action A.

69
00:07:21,339 --> 00:07:27,245
[SPEAKER_01]: All I can do now is look at the nature behind the action B, which in itself, it's not B.

70
00:07:29,447 --> 00:07:30,929
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you can stop doing that as well.

71
00:07:32,304 --> 00:07:36,405
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, eh... like a sotāpanna, he lets go of sīla-bhāta-pāramāsa.

72
00:07:36,485 --> 00:07:38,466
[SPEAKER_01]: He lets go of the of the virtue and duty.

73
00:07:38,606 --> 00:07:41,587
[SPEAKER_01]: I must be doing this as somebody who practices.

74
00:07:41,887 --> 00:07:43,267
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean he becomes immoral.

75
00:07:43,747 --> 00:07:48,989
[SPEAKER_01]: It just means he stops doing it on the level of action and starts developing it on the level of mind.

76
00:07:49,049 --> 00:07:58,651
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why a seka, seka, somebody, a noble disciple, sotāpanna or higher, all he needs to do is keep his virtue unblemished and it will result in arhantship.

77
00:07:59,272 --> 00:08:00,892
[SPEAKER_01]: It will result in, in eh...

78
00:08:01,471 --> 00:08:10,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Freedom from remorse, freedom from remorse results in happiness, happiness, joy, tranquility, and then clear seeing and understanding.

79
00:08:12,135 --> 00:08:19,198
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't just keep the precepts and then expect to end in the... as a result... have as a result the understanding of dhamma.

80
00:08:20,439 --> 00:08:26,681
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to keep the precepts to create a basis of good action, and then on the basis of good wholesome action, you have to see

81
00:08:27,667 --> 00:08:33,409
[SPEAKER_01]: The nature of action, which in itself, it's not that content of action that you're doing.

82
00:08:33,429 --> 00:08:40,492
[SPEAKER_01]: So, abstaining from doing something, does not need to become an action, if you can develop it in that manner.

83
00:08:46,735 --> 00:08:57,079
[SPEAKER_02]: Obviously, people always have some sense of, you know, what is right and what is wrong, regardless of whether they ever really read anything.

84
00:08:57,563 --> 00:09:00,465
[SPEAKER_02]: about ethics or morality or anything like that.

85
00:09:03,887 --> 00:09:04,628
[SPEAKER_02]: And a lot of people...

86
00:09:05,370 --> 00:09:19,518
[SPEAKER_02]: You know, they say that the Buddha and all the other religious people, leaders, taught the same morality, same ethics, like in the Ten Commandments and other such things, like, thou shalt not kill, not steal, those, you know, basic precepts.

87
00:09:20,058 --> 00:09:30,724
[SPEAKER_02]: So, what is it about the Buddha's exhortation about these things that differentiates them from any other person talking about what is good, what is bad?

88
00:09:33,112 --> 00:09:45,379
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, in layman's terms, the difference is that the Buddha tells you to do good, so that eventually, through understanding, you can abandon both good and bad.

89
00:09:46,620 --> 00:09:58,467
[SPEAKER_01]: All the other religions pretty much tell you, you do good, so you get even better, as in, you get reborn in heaven, or you get more of a reward, from Hinduism, Christianity, all the other religions.

90
00:09:58,507 --> 00:09:59,127
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the difference.

91
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:03,982
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha doesn't just tell you to replace bad action with good action and be rewarded for it.

92
00:10:04,782 --> 00:10:10,205
[SPEAKER_01]: He tells you to replace bad action with good action and then understand it fully, that will then truly free you.

93
00:10:10,585 --> 00:10:16,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you might be rewarded for your good action, but that reward will expire.

94
00:10:16,327 --> 00:10:17,188
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't last forever.

95
00:10:17,808 --> 00:10:21,310
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you're back on square one, or even worse.

96
00:10:21,330 --> 00:10:24,551
[SPEAKER_01]: But the Buddha frees you from the bondage of action.

97
00:10:24,571 --> 00:10:26,332
[SPEAKER_01]: The bondage of having to choose.

98
00:10:27,348 --> 00:10:29,827
[SPEAKER_01]: The bondage of having to act and intend.

99
00:10:33,133 --> 00:10:39,518
[SPEAKER_02]: Some other people, especially the non-religious, can have the view that there is no morality.

100
00:10:39,598 --> 00:10:41,299
[SPEAKER_02]: It's like a nihilistic view.

101
00:10:41,620 --> 00:10:48,285
[SPEAKER_02]: It's all relative, that ethics and morals just come from, you know, the laws of society and different cultures.

102
00:10:48,845 --> 00:10:53,869
[SPEAKER_02]: But when we read this, it seems pretty clear, like, you know, killing is unwholesome.

103
00:10:54,530 --> 00:10:59,453
[SPEAKER_02]: Whatever the reasons, the motivation, it's always unwholesome.

104
00:10:59,533 --> 00:11:01,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Existential discrepancy is unwholesome.

105
00:11:02,900 --> 00:11:11,086
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's felt as wrong by anyone, no matter of their background, culture, views, politics, religion.

106
00:11:12,266 --> 00:11:18,110
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what everything that's considered bad, contains that discrepancy.

107
00:11:19,632 --> 00:11:23,934
[SPEAKER_01]: As in true life, you deny life, or someone else's life, which is not yours to deny.

108
00:11:24,815 --> 00:11:29,078
[SPEAKER_01]: Or lying, again, taking that which is not yours, which is not meant for you,

109
00:11:29,630 --> 00:11:41,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Stealing, cheating, harming... Again, all of that causes the discrepancy and people feel that that is the universal morality that can be discerned on the basis of nature of things.

110
00:11:43,455 --> 00:11:56,859
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, people can debate about the specifics of it, but overall, everybody knows deep down whether their action is motivated in overriding the discrepancy and creating and acting out of greed, aversion, delusion or hatred and so on.

111
00:11:59,070 --> 00:12:13,079
[SPEAKER_02]: Sometimes, when it comes to thinking about what's good and bad, people, especially the philosophical mind, would like to start, you know, positing all these theoretical problems, like the philosophical problem, the trolley problem, for example, or kind of...

112
00:12:13,469 --> 00:12:22,375
[SPEAKER_02]: You're playing devil's advocate about like, you know, killing is okay if it's, let's say, someone has a chronic disease and, you know, you're relieving them of pain and suffering.

113
00:12:23,116 --> 00:12:29,420
[SPEAKER_02]: So, is there any value in kind of thinking of these kind of complicated ethical questions or is it just a distraction?

114
00:12:29,701 --> 00:12:30,141
[SPEAKER_01]: All of them.

115
00:12:30,321 --> 00:12:42,670
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a distraction for somebody who wants to practice dhamma, but even if you want to think about them for whatever reason, all of the conclusions that those theoretical questions would arrive at

116
00:12:43,057 --> 00:12:50,602
[SPEAKER_01]: are based on assuming the external point of view of yourself and then making the universal sort of decision which which is impossible.

117
00:12:50,682 --> 00:12:51,503
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable.

118
00:12:52,303 --> 00:13:00,369
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't imagine the experience outside of your experience as a whole, which means you always are bound with your point of view, which means killing is always bad.

119
00:13:02,310 --> 00:13:06,433
[SPEAKER_01]: No amount of external reasoning and the environment can justify it.

120
00:13:10,634 --> 00:13:13,215
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if they say, well, if I don't kill this person, he'll kill hundred.

121
00:13:13,255 --> 00:13:15,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, killing hundred, that's on him.

122
00:13:15,257 --> 00:13:15,937
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not on you.

123
00:13:17,598 --> 00:13:22,220
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, what you're really saying is, oh, I'll kill him, so he doesn't do anything bad in his life.

124
00:13:22,821 --> 00:13:25,042
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'll do something bad, so he doesn't do anything bad.

125
00:13:25,702 --> 00:13:29,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means somebody will need to take responsibility for their killing.

126
00:13:29,344 --> 00:13:32,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, killing is always bad.

127
00:13:32,726 --> 00:13:39,490
[SPEAKER_02]: So, when it comes to discerning wholesome and wholesome, then, it seems like the most important thing is a personal responsibility.

128
00:13:39,797 --> 00:13:40,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Absolutely.

129
00:13:40,557 --> 00:13:41,818
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a necessary thing, yeah.

130
00:13:42,459 --> 00:13:43,059
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what I mean.

131
00:13:43,079 --> 00:13:49,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Through that personal responsibility, you can obviously abstain from doing unwholesome.

132
00:13:49,783 --> 00:13:56,066
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, through accepting responsibility for wholesome, you can start seeing the nature of action in regard to it.

133
00:13:57,307 --> 00:14:00,348
[SPEAKER_01]: As in the necessity of the point of view, the necessity of intentionality.

134
00:14:01,169 --> 00:14:07,032
[SPEAKER_01]: But the non-necessity of appropriation of that action, you cannot see that in unwholesome action,

135
00:14:07,375 --> 00:14:11,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Because by default, it's already perverted based on that discrepancy of appropriation.

136
00:14:12,479 --> 00:14:14,320
[SPEAKER_01]: But wholesome action isn't.

137
00:14:14,340 --> 00:14:18,163
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean that wholesome action is understood.

138
00:14:18,223 --> 00:14:19,204
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what I mean.

139
00:14:19,524 --> 00:14:27,110
[SPEAKER_01]: You abstain from doing good through doing good, but then disowning the ownership of that while you're doing it.

140
00:14:29,052 --> 00:14:30,353
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then it becomes non-doing.

141
00:14:30,993 --> 00:14:35,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you don't really have to do it anymore.

142
00:14:36,558 --> 00:14:36,838
[SPEAKER_01]: Hold on.

143
00:14:38,528 --> 00:14:54,738
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just to add more to that whole thing of ownership, through doing wholesome action, one's motivation can be revealed.

144
00:14:56,219 --> 00:15:07,447
[SPEAKER_01]: And if the motivation itself does not contain that discrepancy, that perversion of the existential discrepancy, whether through killing, lying, stealing, acting out of greed, aversion, delusion,

145
00:15:08,705 --> 00:15:16,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, it can be seen the extent of one's responsibility to that wholesome action that a person does.

146
00:15:17,630 --> 00:15:27,175
[SPEAKER_01]: As an intentionality in itself arises on its own, by you choosing to act upon certain intentions on account of ignoring other intentions, that's on you.

147
00:15:27,835 --> 00:15:34,138
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's something that gets obscured through the habitual action of unwholesome nature.

148
00:15:34,779 --> 00:15:37,340
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot see this background of intentionality behind.

149
00:15:38,915 --> 00:15:44,537
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you uproot the unwholesome intentions, that's when you stop acting, in that sense.

150
00:15:46,597 --> 00:15:56,080
[SPEAKER_02]: There can also be the temptation, you know, with the way they start talking about the cessation of action, and a person just tries to just do as little as they can, basically.

151
00:15:56,100 --> 00:15:58,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, failing to see that that's their action at the time.

152
00:15:58,140 --> 00:16:07,703
[SPEAKER_01]: In a sense, it comes... it boils down to what in the essential philosophy they were saying, like, eh... you are free to choose, but at the same time, you cannot not choose.

153
00:16:08,490 --> 00:16:11,612
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you're condemned to be free.

154
00:16:12,913 --> 00:16:14,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Condemned to be free and condemned to a choice.

155
00:16:15,835 --> 00:16:18,597
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you say, okay, I'm not going to choose anything, you chose that.

156
00:16:19,278 --> 00:16:21,980
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a definition of karma that the Buddha described as action.

157
00:16:22,500 --> 00:16:24,261
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot help but do something.

158
00:16:25,022 --> 00:16:29,505
[SPEAKER_01]: So the only way to stop truly doing anything and choosing is to understand the nature of that choice.

159
00:16:30,583 --> 00:16:42,507
[SPEAKER_01]: The nature of intentionality inherent in it, that has nothing to do with you, and then the degree of your responsibility that you then add to it by choosing this, by intending this on account of that.

160
00:16:43,327 --> 00:16:44,928
[SPEAKER_01]: Choice is an intentional intention.

161
00:16:46,588 --> 00:16:51,230
[SPEAKER_01]: But intention itself, it's not your doing, and does not need to be appropriated.

162
00:16:51,950 --> 00:16:59,813
[SPEAKER_01]: Not knowing that, people appropriate it, and then by default, when then you intentionally intend further your own intentions, it's just, I am doing this.

163
00:17:00,593 --> 00:17:02,215
[SPEAKER_01]: And that does become your action.

164
00:17:03,176 --> 00:17:17,511
